---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const desc = 'Free AI ROI calculator: work out whether an AI rollout pays for itself. Answer eight plain questions and see the hours released, the year-one net, the payback period, and the adoption rate you have to hit.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'AI ROI Calculator', applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/roi/',
});
---
<Base title="AI ROI Calculator: does the rollout pay for itself? — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <p class="meta-line"><a href="/calculators/">AI Calculators</a></p>
  <h1>AI ROI Calculator</h1>

  <p class="answer">An AI rollout pays for itself when the value of the time it saves is worth
    more than the licences, the build, and the running costs. Answer the eight questions below
    and you will see whether it does, how long the build takes to pay back, and the one number
    that decides it more than any other.</p>
  <p>You do not need to know anything about AI to use this. Every question asks for something
    you already know about your own organisation, and each one says where to find the number if
    you are not sure.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-31.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <div class="calc-step">
      <h3><span class="num">1</span>Who is going to use it?</h3>
      <p>Start with the people, not the software. Two numbers: how many are given access, and
        how many of those genuinely use it week to week. They are rarely the same, and the gap
        is where most business cases go wrong.</p>
      <div class="calc-fields">
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="seats">People given access</label>
          <input type="number" id="seats" value="100" step="10" min="1" />
          <span class="hint">Everyone who will hold a licence, whether or not they open it.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="adopt">Of those, the share who use it regularly (%)</label>
          <input type="number" id="adopt" value="40" step="5" min="1" max="100" />
          <span class="hint">Take this from usage reports, not the rollout plan. In most
            organisations it settles well below half.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-step">
      <h3><span class="num">2</span>How much time does it actually save them?</h3>
      <p>Per person, per week, for the people who really use it. Be conservative: a figure you
        can defend to a finance director is worth more than an optimistic one.</p>
      <div class="calc-fields">
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="hours">Hours saved per active user per week</label>
          <input type="number" id="hours" value="2" step="0.5" min="0" />
          <span class="hint">Measured from a pilot if you have one. One to three hours is the
            common honest range for a drafting or search tool.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="realise">Share of that saved time actually put to other work (%)</label>
          <input type="number" id="realise" value="60" step="5" min="0" max="100" />
          <span class="hint">An hour saved only counts if it is spent on something else of
            value. If the day just gets easier, the saving is real for the person and invisible
            in the accounts. 50 to 70 is defensible; 100 almost never is.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-step">
      <h3><span class="num">3</span>What is an hour of their time worth?</h3>
      <p>Use the loaded cost, meaning salary plus employer taxes, benefits and overhead. It is
        usually 1.25 to 1.4 times base pay. Your finance team will have the figure.</p>
      <div class="calc-fields">
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="rate">Loaded cost per hour (USD)</label>
          <input type="number" id="rate" value="75" step="5" min="0" />
          <span class="hint">A $110,000 salary is roughly $75 an hour once loaded, across a
            1,840-hour working year.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-step">
      <h3><span class="num">4</span>What does it cost?</h3>
      <p>Three costs, and people usually remember only the first. The build is what you spend
        once to get it working; the running cost is what it takes to keep it working safely.</p>
      <div class="calc-fields">
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="lic">Licence cost per person per month (USD)</label>
          <input type="number" id="lic" value="30" step="5" min="0" />
          <span class="hint">The per-seat price on the vendor's quote.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="build">One-off build and rollout cost (USD)</label>
          <input type="number" id="build" value="120000" step="10000" min="0" />
          <span class="hint">Integration, data work, security review, training, change
            management. Paid once, in year one.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label for="run">Annual running cost (USD)</label>
          <input type="number" id="run" value="60000" step="10000" min="0" />
          <span class="hint">Support, monitoring, governance and audit, infrastructure, and the
            people who own it. Every year, not just the first.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-answer" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How to read the answer</h2>
  <p><b>Time released</b> is the money value of hours the rollout gives back, after both the
    adoption and the redeployment discounts. It is the benefit side of the case.</p>
  <p><b>Year-one net</b> subtracts everything, including the one-off build, so it is usually
    negative even for a good project. <b>Steady-state net</b> is the year after that, with the
    build behind you, and it is the number that tells you whether the thing is worth running at
    all.</p>
  <p><b>Break-even adoption</b> is the share of your people who must use it regularly for the
    project to wash its face. If that number is higher than adoption you have ever achieved
    with any tool, the case does not work, however good the software is.</p>

  <h2>The two inputs that decide it</h2>
  <p><b>Adoption.</b> Licences bought is not people using. A tool that saves two hours a week
    for the 30 percent who actually use it returns less than a third of what the proposal
    claimed. Measure it from usage telemetry, and revisit it quarterly, because adoption curves
    flatten far below 100 percent in most organisations.</p>
  <p><b>Redeployment.</b> Setting it to 100 percent is the commonest way a business case
    overstates itself. Unless you can point to headcount you did not hire or a backlog you
    cleared, hold it between 50 and 70.</p>

  <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
  <p>This is a labour-saving model: the easiest case to defend and the least complete. It
    excludes revenue gains, quality and error-rate changes, risk and compliance cost, the value
    of decisions made faster, and the cost of a deployment that fails. Those can be larger than
    everything modelled here, in either direction.</p>

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"AI ROI Calculator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/roi/.</code></div>

  <p class="meta-line">This is a planning tool, not financial advice. See our
    <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</p>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var IDS = ['seats', 'adopt', 'hours', 'rate', 'realise', 'lic', 'build', 'run'];
    function n(id) { return parseFloat(document.getElementById(id).value) || 0; }
    function usd(v) { return '$' + Math.round(v).toLocaleString(); }
    function calc() {
      var seats = n('seats'), adopt = n('adopt') / 100, hrs = n('hours');
      var rate = n('rate'), real = n('realise') / 100;
      var lic = n('lic'), build = n('build'), run = n('run');

      var active = seats * adopt;
      var benefit = active * hrs * 46 * rate * real; // 46 working weeks
      var annualCost = seats * lic * 12 + run;
      var steady = benefit - annualCost;
      var firstYear = steady - build;
      var payback = steady > 0 ? build / (steady / 12) : 0;
      var perPoint = seats * 0.01 * hrs * 46 * rate * real;
      var beAdopt = perPoint > 0 ? (annualCost + build) / perPoint : 0;

      var head, verdict;
      if (firstYear > 0) {
        head = 'It pays for itself in the first year';
        verdict = 'Even carrying the whole build cost, this returns <b>' + usd(firstYear) +
          '</b> in year one. Check the adoption figure is one you have actually measured before relying on it.';
      } else if (steady > 0) {
        head = 'It pays back in about ' + payback.toFixed(0) + ' months';
        verdict = 'Year one is negative because the build is paid once and up front. From then on it releases <b>' +
          usd(steady) + '</b> a year, so the build is recovered after roughly <b>' + payback.toFixed(0) +
          ' months</b> of running.';
      } else {
        head = 'It does not pay back at these numbers';
        verdict = 'The time released is worth less than the cost of running it, so the build never comes back. ' +
          'The usual fix is adoption, not the software: you would need <b>' +
          (beAdopt > 100 ? 'more than every person you have' : Math.round(beAdopt) + '% regular use') +
          '</b> to break even.';
      }

      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        '<p class="headline">Your answer</p>' +
        '<p class="big">' + head + '</p>' +
        '<div class="rows">' +
        '<div><span>People actually using it</span><span>' + Math.round(active) + ' of ' + Math.round(seats) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Time released, in money, per year</span><span>' + usd(benefit) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Cost every year</span><span>' + usd(annualCost) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Cost once, to build and roll out</span><span>' + usd(build) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Year one, after everything</span><span>' + usd(firstYear) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Every year after that</span><span>' + usd(steady) + '</span></div>' +
        '<div><span>Regular use needed to break even</span><span>' +
          (beAdopt > 100 ? 'over 100%, unreachable' : Math.round(beAdopt) + '%') + '</span></div>' +
        '</div>' +
        '<p class="verdict">' + verdict + '</p>';
    }
    IDS.forEach(function (i) {
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
</Base>
